---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Glossary terms"
  description="The recurring concepts and gameplay nouns that show up across hundreds of dialog lines, and how we settled on each translation."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'Translation Canon', href: `${base}/field-guide/translation-canon/` },
    { label: 'Glossary' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Some words show up everywhere in the game. The magic-magic-word
    distinction, the names of activities, the time-of-day descriptors.
    Picking the right English for each one matters because the choice
    propagates across thousands of lines.
  </p>

  <h2>Magic vs incantation</h2>
  <p>
    The game uses two closely-related words:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>まほう</strong> (magic): the broad category. Spells, the
      magic class, the magic system in general. Always
      <strong>magic</strong> in English.</li>
    <li><strong>まじない</strong> (literally "spell" or "charm"): used
      for the specific compositions the player creates with the
      magic-glyph keyboard. We translate this as
      <strong>incantation</strong> — it's a more specific word that
      pairs well with the glyph-composition mechanic.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Keeping these distinct matters because a tutorial line might say
    "you've learned a new <em>incantation</em>" referring to a specific
    composition, while another line says "magic class is fun" referring
    to the school subject. The Japanese clearly distinguishes them; so
    do we.
  </p>

  <h2>The magic glyph words</h2>
  <p>
    The 377 individual glyphs each have their own translation —
    covered in <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/magic-glyphs/`}>the
    magic glyph keyboard</a>. A few worth calling out because they
    appear in dialog frequently:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>JP</th><th>EN</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ベリー</td><td>berry</td><td>Game 1 canon.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>きのこ</td><td>mushroom</td><td>Universal.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>くさ</td><td>weeds</td><td>Game 1 canon — note "weeds"
        plural, not "grass."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>むし</td><td>bug</td><td>Singular, matches Game 1's
        insect-catching feature.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>はな</td><td>flower</td><td>Game 1 canon.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>つえ</td><td>staff</td><td>The wizard's staff. Game 1
        canon.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>でんぽう</td><td>telegram</td><td>The in-game messaging
        system.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ふしぎ</td><td>mystery</td><td>Game 1 canon — used for
        in-game lore concepts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ウワサ</td><td>rumor</td><td>From Game 1's magic-word
        list.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>コーデ</td><td>outfit</td><td>The fashion system's
        signature word.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>キュート</td><td>Cute</td><td>One of the fashion-style
        descriptors. Title-cased because it's a category name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>クール</td><td>Cool</td><td>Same category as Cute.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Time of day</h2>
  <p>
    The game runs on the system clock and refers to time of day
    frequently in dialog and quest descriptions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>あさ → <strong>morning</strong></li>
    <li>ひる → <strong>daytime</strong></li>
    <li>よる → <strong>night</strong> (rare in dialog; usually implied
      by event timing)</li>
    <li>いま → <strong>now</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Activities</h2>
  <p>
    The game's repeatable activities — what the player does in town
    every day:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>えんそう → <strong>performance</strong> (music class)</li>
    <li>さつえい → <strong>photo shoot</strong> (the camera
      minigame)</li>
    <li>りょうり → <strong>cooking</strong></li>
    <li>せんでん → <strong>advertise</strong> (for the design
      minigame)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the canon keeps drifting</h2>
  <p>
    Translation choices feel obvious until you find a line where they
    don't fit. We've revised several glossary entries mid-project as
    a context appeared that contradicted the earlier choice. Each
    revision is a one-script-run job because the dialog database has
    canonicalization built in — change the canonical English for a
    term and every affected dialog row updates the next time we
    rebuild.
  </p>

  <p>
    The notes folder in the translation repo tracks the audit history
    if anyone needs to see what the term used to be and why we
    changed it.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
